@Rubegen sorry, I am confused. Can we specify explicitly the timezone when writing the e-mail? On the fake data generator, we must be able to specify the timezone, rather than assume it to be figured implicitly from the user computer. Otherwise, unit tests that check the timezones will be impossible to be written. 

If the fake data generator currently uses the default timezone when constructing the final field only by using the local information of the user computer, then it is impossible to create an example dataset of users collaborating in two different places, unless I force my machine into different timezones, which is impractical.

I am not seeing a way to specify the timezone via the parameter string, but maybe I am be looking on the wrong commit. Let me know if I am misunderstanding what you did.

@Rubegen 

I see some are not (row 4), and some are (row 7). So, if there is inconsistency, my suggestion is that you do not force the double quotes, and let the user of your function decide instead, i.e. your parameter just take a `Firstname Lastname` (please add an example without double quotes on the docs). That being said, it does seem every e-mail adds <>. On your email parameter, tell the user that they can type `example@domain.org` but that your generator will incorporate the `<>`. This should suffice.

Thank you for checking!

